(5619633, 813165, 16912, '<td valign="top">Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club and congratulations on a new #2 image!<br/> <br/><b>First Impression:</b> The two main things that hurt this image were 1. The ,issing head that you mentioned and 2. The Deer is a little dull and dark for me.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> I really like the composition here! The foreground grass is wonderfully blurred, a nice stone to anchor the image, the main deer is placed perfectly and the reflected colours in the water give this shot a warm Summer evening feel.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> Can''t really go wrong with a nice shot of a Deer (or two) and whilst the second Deer is good in the mostpart maybe you would have got that magic 6 score without it? (edited to add no you wouldn''t cos then it wouldn''t be the animals lol)<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> I do like the shot but think its a little flat and maybe playing with the brightness/contrast and even hue/saturation or curves and levels would have helped this pop and brought out more detail in the darkish Deer.<br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts:</b> Obviously a keeper and it must have been a great feeling capturing this shot, still loveing the colours here - well done.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-08-21 18:01:21.</i></td>', 'Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club and congratulations on a new #2 image! First Impression: The two main things that hurt this image were 1. The ,issing head that you mentioned and 2. The Deer is a little dull and dark for me. Composition: I really like the composition here! The foreground grass is wonderfully blurred, a nice stone to anchor the image, the main deer is placed perfectly and the reflected colours in the water give this shot a warm Summer evening feel. Subject: Can''t really go wrong with a nice shot of a Deer (or two) and whilst the second Deer is good in the mostpart maybe you would have got that magic 6 score without it? (edited to add no you wouldn''t cos then it wouldn''t be the animals lol) Technical: I do like the shot but think its a little flat and maybe playing with the brightness/contrast and even hue/saturation or curves and levels would have helped this pop and brought out more detail in the darkish Deer. Final thoughts: Obviously a keeper and it must have been a great feeling capturing this shot, still loveing the colours here - well done.', '2009-08-21 18:59:41.000000', '2009-08-21 19:01:21.000000', 0),
(5620208, 812974, 16912, '<td valign="top">Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club and <b>congratulations</b> on entering your first challenge here at dpc! (FWIW my first score was a 4.2). In case you were wondering the Critique Club is made up of a group of dpchallenge members but each critique is written by an individual not the group.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression:</b> Not heard of the band before and understand the link between title and image however its not a particularly inspiring image for me.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> Whilst there isn''t much to look at here it still appears quite busy, it needs roatating a touch to straighten it and thinking if you had turned to the right some more you would have lost the left building and might have improved the overall feel of the image.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> Whilst you met the challenge there is nothing in the image that I can settle on and keep moving from the blown out sky to the rock at the bottom and distracted by the buildings on both sides.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> OK, the overexposure was important for the challenge, to have scored any better you would have needed to keep colour and detail in the tree and its all a little washed out. As this is the only image you have here its difficult to understand your level of both photography or post processing, either way you will always get great help/tips/critique by posting an image in the individual photograph section of the site. <br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts:</b> Lets call this a benchmark shot for future challenges and since you placed 100th I guess the only way is up. Keep shooting, reading and learning and best of luck for future challenges!</td>', 'Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club and congratulations on entering your first challenge here at dpc! (FWIW my first score was a 4.2). In case you were wondering the Critique Club is made up of a group of dpchallenge members but each critique is written by an individual not the group. First Impression: Not heard of the band before and understand the link between title and image however its not a particularly inspiring image for me. Composition: Whilst there isn''t much to look at here it still appears quite busy, it needs roatating a touch to straighten it and thinking if you had turned to the right some more you would have lost the left building and might have improved the overall feel of the image. Subject: Whilst you met the challenge there is nothing in the image that I can settle on and keep moving from the blown out sky to the rock at the bottom and distracted by the buildings on both sides. Technical: OK, the overexposure was important for the challenge, to have scored any better you would have needed to keep colour and detail in the tree and its all a little washed out. As this is the only image you have here its difficult to understand your level of both photography or post processing, either way you will always get great help/tips/critique by posting an image in the individual photograph section of the site.  Final thoughts: Lets call this a benchmark shot for future challenges and since you placed 100th I guess the only way is up. Keep shooting, reading and learning and best of luck for future challenges!', '2009-08-22 12:55:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(5620489, 812695, 60862, '<td valign="top">Spectacular!  Add another blurred bird shot to my list of favorites.  Congrats on top 10!</td>', 'Spectacular!  Add another blurred bird shot to my list of favorites.  Congrats on top 10!', '2009-08-22 19:32:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(5620600, 812570, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the C Club, <br/> <br/>lol, you did good with this shot, well done<br/> <br/>The tilt is very extreme on this, just a little less tilt would have made people not tilt their heads so much.  But the tilt is good, it took it from a normal boring straight on photo to one that had an excellent POV. <br/> <br/>The yellow is uniform, playing around with the selective colours and colour balance would have helped.  Going along the lines of noise reducer and then going with unsharp mask, may have helped with the background and the folds that you have there. Selective colours in neutral could have made this pop in different colours as well. <br/> <br/>I really like the concept that you have done here, do it again! <br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the C Club,  lol, you did good with this shot, well done The tilt is very extreme on this, just a little less tilt would have made people not tilt their heads so much.  But the tilt is good, it took it from a normal boring straight on photo to one that had an excellent POV.  The yellow is uniform, playing around with the selective colours and colour balance would have helped.  Going along the lines of noise reducer and then going with unsharp mask, may have helped with the background and the folds that you have there. Selective colours in neutral could have made this pop in different colours as well.  I really like the concept that you have done here, do it again!  ', '2009-08-22 22:36:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5620922, 812695, 68769, '<td valign="top">Just stopped by to say that this was my favorite in this challenge, and only 10 given, heck I didn''t even give any 9''s either. Outstanding photograph!!</td>', 'Just stopped by to say that this was my favorite in this challenge, and only 10 given, heck I didn''t even give any 9''s either. Outstanding photograph!!', '2009-08-23 10:14:02.000000', NULL, 0),
